  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "Full paper text is present. It includes flattened tables and figures, but is not cut off or garbled.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "Tests sparse-feature recovery and detection in a constructed overcomplete toy setting, but does not reproduce the original study’s measurement of superposition geometry.",
    "gate_note": "The toy experiment varies the number of sparse ground-truth features relative to D=16 and evaluates SAE feature recovery and detection. These are relevant proxies for recovering features in an overcomplete representation, but the study does not measure learned feature angles, interference, or whether a trained network naturally stores more features than dimensions. Results on scientific image models concern SAE probing usefulness, not the listed LLM or representation-geometry claims. No results from cited work are included.",
    "test_reason": "This is a methods paper proposing Equivariant SAEs. Superposition appears only as motivation. The toy model is synthetic data built by hand: N near-orthonormal vectors in D=16, each active with probability 1/N. No network is trained to store features, so nothing measures whether or how superposition arises. All toy results score SAE variants on recovering imposed features. The probing experiments on vision and scientific models report only downstream usefulness and reconstruction of the authors' method against baselines. None of these results bear on V1–V6 as stated.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that Equivariant SAEs avoid the pitfalls of vanilla SAEs on symmetric data. They find these SAEs learn features more useful for probing despite worse reconstruction, and that reconstruction quality can be inversely correlated with feature usefulness.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study uses a constructed equivariant toy model and evaluates SAE recovery and detection under symmetry; it does not reproduce the original study’s superposition setup or geometry measurements.",
    "method_sensitivity": "In the toy experiment, feature count N varies with D fixed at 16; appendix results also vary orbit size and canonical orbit representatives. In other experiments, results vary with symmetry group, base model, SAE objective, and TopK.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "On synthetic symmetric data with N>D imposed features, vanilla and Archetypal SAEs reconstruct better but recover and detect ground-truth features worse than symmetry-aware SAEs. The authors report reconstruction quality can be inversely correlated with feature usefulness. Shown in Figure 2 only.",
        "proposed_claim": "Under input symmetries, standard SAE dictionaries fail to align with the true generating features, and reconstruction quality is a misleading proxy for feature recovery.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r3",
          "r4"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "n",
